= 1
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

Over the <complex numbers>, the <polynomial factorization> $z^2+1=(z-i)(z+i)$ gives the <partial fraction decomposition>
$$
\frac{z+1}{z^2+1}=\frac{A}{z-i}+\frac{B}{z+i}.
$$
Multiplication by $(z-i)(z+i)$ and comparison of <polynomial> coefficients give $A+B=1$ and $i(A-B)=1$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\frac{z+1}{z^2+1}=\frac{1-i}{2(z-i)}+\frac{1+i}{2(z+i)}},\qquad z\ne\pm i.
$$

= 2
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Substitute $x^3$ into the <Taylor series> for the <exponential function>:
$$
\boxed{e^{x^3}=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{x^{3n}}{n!}}.
$$
The general nonzero term is $x^{3n}/n!$; the <power series> coefficients of powers not divisible by three are zero. The <radius of convergence> is infinite.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <power series> for $e^{y^3}$ has <uniform convergence> on every compact interval, so <termwise integration> from zero to $x$ is valid. Thus
$$
\int_0^x e^{y^3}\,dy=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n!}\int_0^x y^{3n}\,dy
=\boxed{\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{x^{3n+1}}{(3n+1)n!}}.
$$
This <power series> converges for every real $x$, including negative $x$ with the usual orientation of a <definite integral>.

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

On an interval excluding $t=0$, an <integrating factor> for this <first-order linear differential equation> is $t^4$. Multiplying through gives
$$
\frac{d}{dt}(t^4y)=3t^4.
$$
An <antiderivative> then gives $t^4y=3t^5/5+C$, so
$$
\boxed{y(t)=\frac35t+\frac{C}{t^4}},\qquad t\ne0.
$$
The constant is arbitrary on each connected interval of the domain; the original <differential equation> is undefined at zero.

= 4
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use <integration by parts> with $u=(\ln x)^{n+1}$ and $dv=dx$. Since $du=(n+1)(\ln x)^n\,dx/x$, this yields
$$
\boxed{I_{n+1}=x(\ln x)^{n+1}-(n+1)I_n}.
$$
As an identity between <indefinite integrals>, the formula is understood up to an additive constant.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Here $I_0=x$ up to a constant. Substitution of $n=0$ in the <integration by parts> recurrence gives
$$
\boxed{I_1=x\ln x-x+C},\qquad x>0.
$$
Indeed, its <derivative> is $\ln x+1-1=\ln x$.

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <stationary point> has zero <gradient>. The two <partial derivatives> give
$$
f_x=2x-1+y=0,\qquad f_y=x-3-2y=0.
$$
Solving this <system of linear equations> gives
$$
\boxed{(x,y)=(1,-1)},\qquad f(1,-1)=0.
$$

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Hessian matrix> is
$$
H=\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\1&-2\end{pmatrix},\qquad \det H=-5<0.
$$
Its <eigenvalues> have opposite signs, so the <stationary point> is a **<saddle point>**. More directly, putting $x=1+u$ and $y=-1+v$ gives $f=u^2+uv-v^2$: it increases along $v=0$ and decreases along $u=0$.

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <inclusion-exclusion principle for probabilities> gives
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
P(A\cup B\cup C)={}&P(A)+P(B)+P(C)\\
&-P(A\cap B)-P(A\cap C)-P(B\cap C)\\
&+P(A\cap B\cap C).
\end{aligned}}
$$
Each outcome belonging to two events is initially counted twice and must lose one count. An outcome in all three is counted three times and then subtracted three times, so the last term restores its single count.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $U=A\cup B\cup C$. Since $D\subseteq U$, their <intersection> is $U\cap D=D$. Therefore the definition of <conditional probability> gives
$$
\boxed{P(U\mid D)=\frac{P(U\cap D)}{P(D)}=1},\qquad P(D)>0.
$$
The positive-probability condition is necessary: if $P(D)=0$, this elementary definition of <conditional probability> does not assign a value.

= 7
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

This is a <generalized eigenvalue problem>. A nonzero <vector> lies in the <kernel> of $A-\lambda B$ precisely when its <determinant> vanishes:
$$
0=\det\begin{pmatrix}1&2-2\lambda\\2-2\lambda&1\end{pmatrix}
=1-4(1-\lambda)^2.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{\lambda=\frac12\quad\text{or}\quad\lambda=\frac32}.
$$
The corresponding <eigenvectors> can be taken as $(1,-1)^T$ and $(1,1)^T$, respectively, which also checks that both values give nontrivial solutions.

= 8
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

Use the <product-to-sum formula> and the double-angle <trigonometric identities>:
$$
\cos^3x\sin x=\frac14(1+\cos2x)\sin2x
=\frac14\sin2x+\frac18\sin4x.
$$
By <Fourier orthogonality>, these are already its only nonzero <Fourier series> coefficients:
$$
\boxed{a_n=0\ (n\geq0),\qquad b_2=\frac14,\quad b_4=\frac18,\quad b_n=0\ (n\ne2,4)}.
$$

= 9
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

In <spherical coordinates>, integrating over the angular variables gives the <volume element> $4\pi r^2\,dr$ for a spherical shell. The <mass density> therefore gives the total <mass>
$$
M=4\pi\int_0^a(1+r)r^2\,dr
=4\pi\left[\frac{r^3}{3}+\frac{r^4}{4}\right]_0^a.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{M=\frac{4\pi a^3}{3}+\pi a^4}.
$$

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For the outward orientation of a <spherical cap>, its <vector area element> is
$$
d\mathbf S=\hat{\mathbf r}\,a^2\sin\theta\,d\theta\,d\phi,\qquad
\hat{\mathbf r}=(\sin\theta\cos\phi,\sin\theta\sin\phi,\cos\theta).
$$
In the <surface integral> defining the <vector area>, the $x$ and $y$ components vanish by integration over a complete azimuth. The remaining component is
$$
S_z=2\pi a^2\int_0^{\pi/4}\cos\theta\sin\theta\,d\theta
=\frac{\pi a^2}{2}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\mathbf S=\left(0,0,\frac{\pi a^2}{2}\right)}.
$$
This also equals the area of the cap's projection onto the horizontal plane, directed upwards.

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The direction must first be represented by a <unit vector>: $\hat{\mathbf q}=(1,1,1)/\sqrt3$. The <scalar projection> of the <vector area> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbf S\cdot\hat{\mathbf q}=\frac{\pi a^2}{2\sqrt3}}.
$$
If a <vector projection> is intended, multiply this scalar by $\hat{\mathbf q}$:
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf q}\mathbf S=\frac{\pi a^2}{6}(1,1,1)}.
$$

