{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Choose <Cartesian coordinates> with <unit vectors> satisfying the <right-hand rule>, $\hat{\mathbf x}\times\hat{\mathbf y}=\hat{\mathbf z}$. Put $A$, $B$ and $C$ one unit along the positive $x$, $y$ and $z$ axes, respectively. The following original drawing also includes the vectors needed in the next part.

\Image[paper-2-q11-basis.png]
{title=Cartesian axes and three independent midpoint position vectors}

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <position vectors> end at the midpoints of $BC$, $CA$ and $AB$, respectively. The drawing places $E$, $F$ and $G$ on those edges and shows the arrows from the origin. The volume of their <parallelepiped> is the absolute <scalar triple product>:
$$
\mathbf e\times\mathbf f=\left(\frac14,\frac14,-\frac14\right),\qquad
V=|(\mathbf e\times\mathbf f)\cdot\mathbf g|=\boxed{\frac14}.
$$
Equivalently, this is the absolute <determinant> of the matrix whose columns are the three vectors. Since it is nonzero, the vectors are <linearly independent>. Three <linearly independent> vectors in the three-dimensional <vector space> $\mathbb R^3$ form a <basis>.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Writing $\overrightarrow{OA}=a\mathbf e+b\mathbf f+c\mathbf g$ and comparing <Cartesian coordinates> gives
$$
\frac{b+c}{2}=1,\qquad \frac{a+c}{2}=0,\qquad \frac{a+b}{2}=0.
$$
The unique solution, guaranteed by the <basis> property, is $(a,b,c)=(-1,1,1)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\overrightarrow{OA}=-\mathbf e+\mathbf f+\mathbf g}.
$$

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Two directions within the plane are
$$
\mathbf f-\mathbf e=\left(\frac12,-\frac12,0\right),\qquad
\mathbf g-\mathbf e=\left(\frac12,0,-\frac12\right).
$$
Their <cross product> is $(1,1,1)/4$, so a <unit vector> normal to the plane is
$$
\boxed{\hat{\mathbf n}=\frac{(1,1,1)}{\sqrt3}}.
$$
Its negative is equally valid. An <equation of a plane through three points> in vector form is
$$
\boxed{\hat{\mathbf n}\cdot(\mathbf r-\mathbf e)=0},
$$
or, parametrically, $\mathbf r=\mathbf e+s(\mathbf f-\mathbf e)+t(\mathbf g-\mathbf e)$. Since the coordinate sum of $E$ is one, the <Cartesian equation of a plane> is
$$
\boxed{x+y+z=1}.
$$
The <distance from a point to a plane> is the absolute normal displacement, giving
$$
\boxed{d(O,EFG)=|\hat{\mathbf n}\cdot\mathbf e|=\frac1{\sqrt3}}.
$$

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $\mathbf d=\mathbf f-\mathbf e$. A point on the line has <position vector> $\mathbf r=\mathbf e+t\mathbf d$. By bilinearity and antisymmetry of the <cross product>,
$$
\boxed{\mathbf r\times\mathbf d=\mathbf e\times\mathbf d+t\mathbf d\times\mathbf d=\mathbf e\times\mathbf f}.
$$
For a point with <position vector> $\mathbf c$, the area of the <parallelogram> spanned by $\mathbf c-\mathbf e$ and $\mathbf d$ equals base length times perpendicular <Euclidean distance>. Hence the shortest <Euclidean distance> to the line is
$$
\boxed{d=\frac{|(\mathbf c-\mathbf e)\times(\mathbf f-\mathbf e)|}{|\mathbf f-\mathbf e|}}.
$$
For $\mathbf c=(0,0,1)$, these quantities are
$$
(\mathbf c-\mathbf e)\times(\mathbf f-\mathbf e)=\left(\frac14,\frac14,\frac14\right),\qquad
|\mathbf f-\mathbf e|=\frac1{\sqrt2}.
$$
Thus $\boxed{d=\sqrt6/4}$. The perpendicular meets $EF$ at $(1/4,1/4,1/2)$, whose displacement from $C$ has precisely this length.

= 12
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <gradient> vanishes when
$$
f_x=x(1+2y)=0,\qquad f_y=y+x^2-y^2=0.
$$
If $x=0$, the second equation gives $y=0$ or $y=1$. If $y=-1/2$, it gives $x^2=3/4$. These exhaust the possibilities. The <stationary points> and their values are
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c}
(x,y)&f(x,y)\\\hline
(0,0)&0\\
(0,1)&1/6\\
(\sqrt3/2,-1/2)&1/6\\
(-\sqrt3/2,-1/2)&1/6
\end{array}}.
$$

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Hessian matrix> is
$$
H(x,y)=\begin{pmatrix}1+2y&2x\\2x&1-2y\end{pmatrix}.
$$
At the origin it is the identity, so the origin is a strict **<local minimum>**. At $(0,1)$ it is $\operatorname{diag}(3,-1)$, so this point is a **<saddle point>**. At either remaining <stationary point>,
$$
H=\begin{pmatrix}0&\pm\sqrt3\\\pm\sqrt3&2\end{pmatrix},\qquad \det H=-3<0,
$$
which again gives a **<saddle point>**. All three <saddle points> have <eigenvalues> $3$ and $-1$; there are no <local maxima>.

= c
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The most useful guide to the <contour plot> is the common saddle value. Direct <polynomial factorization> gives
$$
6f-1=(2y+1)\bigl[3x^2-(y-1)^2\bigr].
$$
Thus the <level set> $f=1/6$ is exactly the three complete lines
$$
\boxed{y=-\frac12,\qquad y=1+\sqrt3x,\qquad y=1-\sqrt3x}.
$$
Their intersections are the three <saddle points>, and they enclose the origin in an equilateral triangle. Near the <local minimum>, the <level sets> are nearly circles because the leading term is $(x^2+y^2)/2$. For $0<c<1/6$, a closed contour surrounds the origin, while three separate open branches lie outside the triangular barrier. At the saddle level these components meet at the <saddle points>; above it the central closed contour has opened into the three outer channels.

The identity in <polar coordinates>
$$
f=\frac12r^2+\frac13r^3\sin3\theta
$$
shows the rotation symmetry through $2\pi/3$. It identifies the function as the <Hénon-Heiles potential> and helps organize all three channels in the original sketch below. The dashed straight lines are the saddle level; labels on the other contours give $f$.

\Image[paper-2-q12-contours.png]
{title=Contours and gradient directions of the Hénon-Heiles potential}

= d
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

At each regular point, the <gradient>
$$
\boxed{\nabla f=\bigl(x(1+2y),\ y+x^2-y^2\bigr)}
$$
is normal to the <level set> and points towards increasing $f$. The arrows in the <contour plot> above show this direction; their lengths have been normalized to keep the outer arrows legible. Near the origin they point outwards, as expected for a <local minimum>. Near $(0,1)$, write $\eta=y-1$; the linear approximation is $\nabla f\simeq(3x,-\eta)$. Thus the arrows point away along the horizontal direction and towards the point along the vertical direction. The other two <saddle points> have the same behaviour after rotations through $2\pi/3$. All four <stationary points> have zero <gradient>, so no direction arrow is assigned at the points themselves.

= 13
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <line integral> uses $\mathbf F(\mathbf r(t))\cdot\mathbf r'(t)$, including the tangent vector. With $\mathbf r(t)=(t,t,t)$, $0\leq t\leq1$, the three components sum to
$$
\mathbf F(\mathbf r(t))\cdot\mathbf r'(t)=(4\alpha+2\alpha^2+\beta)t+\beta t^2.
$$
Consequently
$$
\int\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
=\frac{4\alpha+2\alpha^2+\beta}{2}+\frac\beta3
=\boxed{\alpha^2+2\alpha+\frac56\beta}.
$$

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

It is helpful first to separate an exact <gradient>:
$$
\mathbf F=\nabla\bigl(\alpha x^2+\alpha^2xy+\alpha xz\bigr)+(0,\beta z,\beta y^2).
$$
The <fundamental theorem for line integrals> makes the gradient contribution $2\alpha+\alpha^2$ for either path. On the curved path, $dy=2t\,dt$, $dz=3t^2\,dt$, $y=t^2$ and $z=t^3$, so the remaining contribution is
$$
\beta\int_0^1(z\,dy+y^2\,dz)=\beta\int_0^1(2t^4+3t^6)\,dt=\frac{29\beta}{35}.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\int\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r=\alpha^2+2\alpha+\frac{29\beta}{35}}.
$$

= c
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A continuously differentiable <conservative vector field> has zero <curl>. Here
$$
\nabla\times\mathbf F=
\left(\frac{\partial F_z}{\partial y}-\frac{\partial F_y}{\partial z},\frac{\partial F_x}{\partial z}-\frac{\partial F_z}{\partial x},\frac{\partial F_y}{\partial x}-\frac{\partial F_x}{\partial y}\right)
=\bigl(\beta(2y-1),0,0\bigr).
$$
This vanishes everywhere if and only if $\boxed{\beta=0}$. Since the field is smooth on the <simply connected> domain $\mathbb R^3$, zero <curl> is also sufficient; the explicit <scalar potential> in the next part confirms it.

= d
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

With $\beta=0$, integrating $\Phi_x=2\alpha x+\alpha^2y+\alpha z$ gives
$$
\Phi=\alpha x^2+\alpha^2xy+\alpha xz+K(y,z).
$$
The other two <partial derivatives> require $K_y=K_z=0$. The origin condition fixes the remaining constant to zero. Hence the <scalar potential> is
$$
\boxed{\Phi(x,y,z)=\alpha x^2+\alpha^2xy+\alpha xz}.
$$
Its value at $(1,1,1)$ gives $\alpha^2+2\alpha=1$, so
$$
\boxed{\alpha=-1\pm\sqrt2}.
$$

= 14
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a continuous <random variable>, the <probability density> integrates to the probability of an interval:
$$
\boxed{P(a\leq X\leq b)=\int_a^b f(x)\,dx}.
$$
Individual endpoints have zero probability, so strict or non-strict inequalities give the same answer.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Subtracting the constant translates the event without changing its probability. Therefore
$$
\boxed{P(a\leq U\leq b)=P(a-c\leq X\leq b-c)=\int_{a-c}^{b-c}f(x)\,dx}.
$$
This is a translation of the interval in the <probability distribution> of $X$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Apply the preceding interval formula on a short interval and use the <change of variables> $v=x+c$:
$$
P(u\leq U\leq u+du)=\int_{u-c}^{u+du-c}f(x)\,dx
=\int_u^{u+du}f(v-c)\,dv.
$$
Comparison with the defining integral for a <probability density> gives
$$
\boxed{t(u)=f(u-c)}.
$$
At continuity points, this can also be read as $P(u\leq U\leq u+du)=f(u-c)\,du+o(du)$.

= c
{parent=14}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <independence of random variables> means that fixing $Y=y$ leaves the <conditional probability density> of $X$ equal to $f$. Given that value of $Y$, $Z$ is the translate $X+y$. Hence
$$
\boxed{P(z\leq Z\leq z+dz\mid Y=y)=\int_{z-y}^{z+dz-y}f(x)\,dx}.
$$
At a continuity point of $f$, the expression is $f(z-y)\,dz+o(dz)$. Since $P(Y=y)=0$, this conditioning uses a <conditional probability density>, rather than division by the probability of the single event $Y=y$.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the <law of total probability> to average the conditional interval probability over $Y$:
$$
P(z\leq Z\leq z+dz)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}g(y)\int_z^{z+dz}f(v-y)\,dv\,dy.
$$
The integrands are nonnegative, so <Tonelli's theorem> permits interchanging the <integrals>. Thus
$$
P(z\leq Z\leq z+dz)=\int_z^{z+dz}\left[\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}g(y)f(v-y)\,dy\right]dv.
$$
Reading off the <probability density> gives the <convolution of probability densities>:
$$
\boxed{h(z)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}g(y)f(z-y)\,dy}.
$$

= d
{parent=14}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <uniform distribution> has constant <probability density> on its interval, and the integral of that density must be one. The interval has length one, so
$$
\boxed{g(y)=\begin{cases}1,&-1/2\leq y\leq1/2,\\0,&\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}}
$$
Changing either endpoint value has no effect on the <probability distribution>.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Insert the two densities into the <convolution of probability densities>:
$$
h(z)=\frac1\pi\int_{-1/2}^{1/2}\frac{dy}{1+(z-y)^2}.
$$
Using the <antiderivative> $-\arctan(z-y)$ with respect to $y$ gives
$$
\boxed{h(z)=\frac{\arctan(z+1/2)-\arctan(z-1/2)}{\pi}},\qquad z\in\mathbb R.
$$
This is the <convolution of a Cauchy distribution and a uniform distribution> with half-width $1/2$.

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <probability density> is an <even function>. Its <derivative> simplifies to
$$
h'(z)=\frac1\pi\left[\frac1{1+(z+1/2)^2}-\frac1{1+(z-1/2)^2}\right]
=\frac{-2z}{\pi[1+(z+1/2)^2][1+(z-1/2)^2]}.
$$
It increases for $z<0$ and decreases for $z>0$, with maximum height $h(0)=2\arctan(1/2)/\pi$. Thus the most probable value, or <mode>, is $\boxed{z=0}$. The original sketch shows the symmetric, slightly flattened peak and long tails.

\Image[paper-2-q14-cauchy-smoothing.png]
{title=Density of a standard Cauchy variable after an independent uniform displacement}

There is a genuine qualification to the request for a mean: **the mean does not exist**. The integral representation shows that $h(z)\sim1/(\pi z^2)$ as $|z|\to\infty$, so
$$
\int_0^\infty z h(z)\,dz=\infty,\qquad
\int_{-\infty}^0 z h(z)\,dz=-\infty.
$$
The two divergent integrals cannot be subtracted to define an <expected value>. Symmetry does give the <Cauchy principal value> $\lim_{A\to\infty}\int_{-A}^A zh(z)\,dz=0$, but that is not the mean. Equivalently, adding the bounded variable $Y$ cannot make the first absolute moment of a <Cauchy distribution> finite.

= 15
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <characteristic equation> of the <homogeneous linear differential equation> is
$$
m^2-4m+13=0,\qquad m=2\pm3i.
$$
The real <general solution> is $y=e^{2x}(A\cos3x+B\sin3x)$. The first boundary value gives $A=\pi$. At $x=-\pi/2$, $\cos(-3\pi/2)=0$ and $\sin(-3\pi/2)=1$, so $1=e^{-\pi}B$. Hence
$$
\boxed{y(x)=e^{2x}\bigl(\pi\cos3x+e^\pi\sin3x\bigr)}.
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <characteristic equation> has a repeated root $-1$, suggesting the substitution $y=e^{-x}v$. By the <product rule>,
$$
y'=e^{-x}(v'-v),\qquad y''=e^{-x}(v''-2v'+v),
$$
so $y''+2y'+y=e^{-x}v''$. The <inhomogeneous linear differential equation> reduces to $v''=2x$. Two integrations give
$$
v=\frac{x^3}{3}+C_1x+C_2.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{y(x)=e^{-x}\left(\frac{x^3}{3}+C_1x+C_2\right)}.
$$
The two arbitrary constants account for the repeated-root <homogeneous solution>.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The first equation gives $v=u'+3u$. Differentiate it and eliminate $v'$ using the second equation:
$$
u''=-3u'+v'=-3u'-5u+v=-3u'-5u+u'+3u.
$$
Thus the required <second-order linear differential equation> is
$$
\boxed{u''+2u'+2u=0}.
$$
Conversely, a solution of this equation together with $v=u'+3u$ solves both original equations, so the elimination loses no solutions.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <characteristic equation> is $m^2+2m+2=0$, with roots $-1\pm i$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{u(t)=e^{-t}(A\cos t+B\sin t)}.
$$
If the second component is also wanted, the relation $v=u'+3u$ gives
$$
v(t)=e^{-t}\bigl[(2A+B)\cos t+(2B-A)\sin t\bigr].
$$
These are damped oscillations associated with the complex conjugate <eigenvalues> of the coefficient matrix.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The first <initial condition> gives $A=1$. The original first equation gives $u'(0)=-3u(0)+v(0)=-2$, whereas the <general solution> gives $u'(0)=B-A$. Thus $B=-1$ and
$$
\boxed{u(t)=e^{-t}(\cos t-\sin t)}.
$$
The corresponding $v=e^{-t}(\cos t-3\sin t)$ has $v(0)=1$, checking the other <initial condition>.

