= Solution
{parent=a}

Each of the three surface pieces is invariant under $y\mapsto-y$, while $y^3$ is an <odd function> under that reflection. Its <surface integral> therefore vanishes on each piece separately. Only $z$ contributes.

On the cylindrical side, use <cylindrical coordinates> $x=a\cos\phi$, $y=a\sin\phi$, with <area element> $dS=a\,d\phi\,dz$:
$$
I_{\mathrm{side}}=\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^b az\,dz\,d\phi=\pi ab^2.
$$
The upper disc has constant $z=b$ and area $\pi a^2$, giving $I_{\mathrm{top}}=\pi a^2b$. On the hemispherical indentation, use <spherical coordinates> with $z=a\cos\theta$ and $dS=a^2\sin\theta\,d\theta\,d\phi$:
$$
I_{\mathrm{hemisphere}}=2\pi a^3\int_0^{\pi/2}\cos\theta\sin\theta\,d\theta=\pi a^3.
$$
Adding the three contributions yields
$$
\boxed{\iint_S(z+y^3)\,dS=\pi a(b^2+ab+a^2)}.
$$
This is a scalar <surface integral>, so the indentation contributes positively through $dS$; an inward or outward orientation does not change its sign.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $q(x)=1/[x(1+(\ln x)^2)]$. The <vector field> is undefined at $x=0$ because $q$ diverges there, but this component is tangential to that face. The face fluxes have finite <improper integral> limits. In particular, the substitution $s=\ln x$ gives
$$
J=\int_0^1q(x)\,dx=\int_{-\infty}^0\frac{ds}{1+s^2}=\frac\pi2.
$$
Using the outward <unit normal> on each face, the <flux> contributions are
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{face}&\mathbf F\cdot\hat{\mathbf n}&\text{outward flux}\\\hline
x=0&-1\text{ (normal trace)}&-1\\
x=1&e^{-1}&e^{-1}\\
y=0&-q(x)&-\pi/2\\
y=1&q(x)&\pi/2\\
z=0&0&0\\
z=1&1&1
\end{array}}
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{\text{total outward flux}=e^{-1}}.
$$
For a precise justification at the singular face, first use the truncated cube $\varepsilon\leq x\leq1$. Its opposite $y$-face integrals cancel, the $x$-faces contribute $e^{-1}-e^{-\varepsilon}$, and the upper $z$-face contributes $1-\varepsilon$. The limit as $\varepsilon\downarrow0$ is $e^{-1}$. On each truncated cube the <divergence theorem> gives the same answer, since $\nabla\cdot\mathbf F=1-e^{-x}$. Thus the result is a <flux of a vector field with an integrable tangential singularity>, rather than an application of the classical smooth-field theorem directly at $x=0$.

