The <determinant> is multiplicative under <matrix multiplication>:
$$
\boxed{|AB|=|A|\,|B|}.
$$
This also holds when $B$ is singular, in which case both sides vanish.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Multiplying an $n\times n$ matrix by $\alpha$ scales every row, giving a factor $\alpha^n$ in its <determinant>. Also $|A^{-1}|=1/|A|$, since $AA^{-1}=I$. Hence
$$
\boxed{|\alpha A^{-1}|=\frac{\alpha^n}{|A|}}.
$$
Only $A$ needs to be invertible; the result remains valid when $\alpha=0$.

