For period two, the <Fourier series> convention is
$$
f(x)=\frac{a_0}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\bigl[a_n\cos(n\pi x)+b_n\sin(n\pi x)\bigr].
$$
Since $f$ is an <even function>, every $b_n$ is zero. Also $a_0=\int_{-1}^1\cosh x\,dx=2\sinh1$. For $n\geq1$, an <antiderivative> useful for the cosine coefficient is
$$
\int\cosh x\cos(n\pi x)\,dx=
\frac{\sinh x\cos(n\pi x)+n\pi\cosh x\sin(n\pi x)}{1+n^2\pi^2}.
$$
Evaluation at $\pm1$ gives $a_n=2(-1)^n\sinh1/(1+n^2\pi^2)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\sinh1\left[1+2\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^n\cos(n\pi x)}{1+n^2\pi^2}\right]}.
$$
The periodic extension is continuous at $x=1$, so its <Fourier series> converges to $\cosh1$ there. Since $\cos(n\pi)=(-1)^n$,
$$
\cosh1=\sinh1\left[1+2\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac1{1+n^2\pi^2}\right].
$$
Therefore $\boxed{p=2,\ q=\pi}$, with $q=-\pi$ giving the same expression because only $q^2$ occurs. This is the $\beta=1$ case of <Fourier series of periodic hyperbolic sine and cosine>.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

On the open interval, $g=f'$. It is an <odd function> there, so its constant and cosine coefficients vanish; assigned values at the endpoints do not affect these <integrals>. For its sine coefficient, <integration by parts> gives
$$
b_n^{(g)}=\int_{-1}^1 f'(x)\sin(n\pi x)\,dx
=\left[f(x)\sin(n\pi x)\right]_{-1}^1-n\pi\int_{-1}^1 f(x)\cos(n\pi x)\,dx
=-n\pi a_n.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{g(x)=2\pi\sinh1\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^{n+1}n\sin(n\pi x)}{1+n^2\pi^2}},\qquad -1<x<1.
$$
The endpoint qualification matters. The periodic $g$ has a <jump discontinuity> at every odd integer, with one-sided limits $\pm\sinh1$. By the <Dirichlet-Jordan convergence theorem>, the <Fourier series> converges to their average, zero, there, not to the assigned value $g(1)=\sinh1$. The <integration by parts> argument also avoids assuming unrestricted termwise differentiation of the first series.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For the period-two <Fourier series> of a <square-integrable function> $w$, <Parseval's identity> states
$$
\int_{-1}^1|w(x)|^2\,dx=\frac{|a_0|^2}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\bigl(|a_n|^2+|b_n|^2\bigr).
$$
For $w=f-g$, write $s=\sinh1$. Its coefficients are $a_0=2s$, $a_n=2(-1)^ns/(1+n^2\pi^2)$ and $b_n=n\pi a_n$, because $g$ is subtracted. Therefore
$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_{-1}^1(f-g)^2\,dx
&=2s^2+4s^2\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{1+n^2\pi^2}{(1+n^2\pi^2)^2}\\
&=2s^2\left[1+2\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac1{1+n^2\pi^2}\right]\\
&=2\sinh1\cosh1=\boxed{\sinh2}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The sum was obtained in part (a). As a separate check, $f-g=e^{-x}$ in the interval, whose square has integral $\int_{-1}^1e^{-2x}\,dx=\sinh2$. Endpoint jumps have no effect on this <integral>.

