= Solution
{parent=a}

For a closed <circular cylinder>, $S=2\pi r^2+2\pi rh$ and the volume constraint is $\pi r^2h=V$. Use a <Lagrange multiplier>:
$$
\mathcal L=2\pi r^2+2\pi rh-\lambda(\pi r^2h-V).
$$
The stationary equations are
$$
4\pi r+2\pi h-2\lambda\pi rh=0,\qquad 2\pi r-\lambda\pi r^2=0.
$$
For $r>0$, the second gives $\lambda=2/r$; the first then gives $h=2r$. The constraint yields
$$
\boxed{r=\left(\frac{V}{2\pi}\right)^{1/3},\qquad h=2\left(\frac{V}{2\pi}\right)^{1/3}},
$$
and
$$
\boxed{S_{\min}=6\pi\left(\frac{V}{2\pi}\right)^{2/3}}.
$$
To verify a global <minimum>, eliminate $h$ to obtain $S(r)=2\pi r^2+2V/r$. It tends to infinity at both ends of $r>0$ and has $S''(r)=4\pi+4V/r^3>0$, so its unique <stationary point> is the global <minimum>.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The base lies in the plane $z=R-h$. Its circular rim lies on the <sphere>, so
$$
r^2+(R-h)^2=R^2,\qquad 0\leq h\leq2R.
$$
Maximize the <cone> volume $V_c=\pi r^2h/3$ using the <Lagrange multiplier> expression
$$
\mathcal L=\frac\pi3r^2h-\lambda\bigl[r^2+(R-h)^2-R^2\bigr].
$$
For a nondegenerate <cone>, the $r$ equation gives $\lambda=\pi h/3$. The $h$ equation is $\pi r^2/3+2\lambda(R-h)=0$, hence $r^2=2h(h-R)$. Combining this with $r^2=2Rh-h^2$ gives
$$
\boxed{h=\frac{4R}{3},\qquad r=\frac{2\sqrt2}{3}R}.
$$
Indeed, eliminating $r$ gives $V_c(h)=\pi h^2(2R-h)/3$, which vanishes at the endpoints and has a unique positive interior maximum at $4R/3$. Thus
$$
\boxed{V_{c,\max}=\frac{32\pi R^3}{81},\qquad
\frac{V_{c,\max}}{(4/3)\pi R^3}=\frac8{27}}.
$$

= c
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Because all variables are positive, the product constraint is equivalent to $\sum_i\ln x_i=\ln a$. The <Lagrange multiplier> equations for
$$
\mathcal L=\sum_{i=1}^n x_i-\lambda\left(\sum_{i=1}^n\ln x_i-\ln a\right)
$$
are $1-\lambda/x_i=0$. Thus every $x_i$ equals $\lambda$, and the constraint requires $\lambda=a^{1/n}$. The candidate <minimum> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\min\sum_{i=1}^n x_i=na^{1/n},\qquad x_1=\cdots=x_n=a^{1/n}}.
$$
For a global justification, put $y_i=\ln x_i$. The objective becomes the strictly <convex function> $\sum_i e^{y_i}$ on the affine constraint $\sum_i y_i=\ln a$. A stationary point of this restricted <convex function> is its unique global <minimum>, establishing the claim without presupposing the desired inequality.

Finally take $a=\prod_i x_i$ and divide the minimum bound by $n$. This is the <arithmetic-geometric mean inequality>:
$$
\boxed{\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\geq\left(\prod_{i=1}^n x_i\right)^{1/n}},
$$
with equality exactly when all the positive variables are equal.

